<ModellingLayout title="Explore the model" description="Learn the distinctions that make property information usable: which subject, role, quantity, time, claim or meaning are we actually describing? Start with the question on your desk." proseClass="prose wide modelling-document learning-document">
  <p>A model is easier to inspect when every statement has something recognisable behind it. “This report describes that inspection of Flat 1” is an account of a situation, not a diagramming exercise. The chapters here help you ask what the account preserves and what a reader would have to guess.</p>
  <p>You do not need to work through them in order. If you are comparing two floor areas, begin with measurements. If a choice list is causing confusion, begin with names and choices. Each chapter supplies the facts its worked cases need and makes the reasoning visible.</p>

  <h2 id="different-questions">The same situation invites different questions</h2>
  <p>A dwelling is something we identify. A report is itself an identifiable thing, a description and potentially evidence. A review decision says what happened to a proposal, not whether the dwelling's condition is true. These are perspectives on statements, not mutually exclusive boxes into which every object must fit.</p>
  <p>The useful habit is to name the aspect you mean. A corrected report can change the available evidence without changing the inspection event. A role can change in one arrangement while the same person participates differently in another. A model makes those distinctions explicit enough to inspect.</p>

  <h2 id="chapters">Find the distinction you need</h2>
  <SubpageCards labelledby="chapters">
    <li><a class="card" href="/semantic-modelling/explore/things-and-identities"><h3>Things, identities and relationships</h3><p>What exactly is being described? Separate a dwelling from its building, address and documents; read the meaning and direction of a connection.</p></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/semantic-modelling/explore/people-roles-and-change"><h3>People, roles and change</h3><p>Who participates, in which arrangement? Compare multiple roles, listing states, report corrections and new events.</p></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/semantic-modelling/explore/measurements-amounts-and-values"><h3>Measurements, amounts and values</h3><p>What does the number mean? Explain an area inclusion difference and distinguish an asking price, transaction price and valuation opinion.</p></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/semantic-modelling/explore/dates-periods-and-applicability"><h3>Dates, periods and applicability</h3><p>What does the date locate? Keep an event's occurrence separate from document issue, recording and a statement's applicability.</p></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/semantic-modelling/explore/claims-evidence-and-uncertainty"><h3>Claims, evidence and uncertainty</h3><p>What supports the statement? Follow sources and distinguish missing, unknown, inapplicable, withheld, negative and conflicting information.</p></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/semantic-modelling/explore/names-and-choices"><h3>Names, choices and classification</h3><p>What do the words and choices mean? Inspect labels, definitions, closed lists, topics and independent facets.</p></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/semantic-modelling/explore/contexts-and-connections"><h3>Connect meanings across contexts</h3><p>Where does meaning belong? Use the established semantic homes and test what a proposed correspondence preserves or loses.</p></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/semantic-modelling/explore/rules-and-exceptions"><h3>Rules, requirements and exceptions</h3><p>When does a requirement apply? Distinguish an unmet requirement from a bad rule or missing evidence.</p></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/semantic-modelling/explore/sensitivity-purpose-permissions"><h3>Sensitivity, purpose and permissions</h3><p>Which handling distinctions matter? Separate participation, purpose and scoped permission descriptions from downstream enforcement.</p></a></li>
  </SubpageCards>

  <h2 id="read-a-connection">Read a connection as a sentence</h2>
  <p>“Flat 1 — report version 2” makes the reader guess. “Report version 2 describes the inspection concerning Flat 1” names the report, event and dwelling and says how they connect. Now ask what else the statement needs: a source, period, measurement basis or arrangement?</p>
  <Callout title="A small reading exercise" tone="method" heading="h3" id="starting-practice">
    <p>“The latest report proves that the property has not changed.” What would you ask before relying on it?</p>
    <p><strong>Worked response:</strong> identify which property, report version and kind of change the sentence means, then inspect what the report actually supports. A recent issue date does not establish a recent visit or an unchanged dwelling. This claim needs evidence, not merely a more confident label.</p>
  </Callout>
  <h2 id="layers-of-detail">Use the explanation, then put it to work</h2>
  <p>The cases are fictional teaching accounts, with explicit premises and visible answers. Some support a definite conclusion; others deliberately leave an evidence gap. They are not candidate implementation claims or professional rules. Formal representation choices remain in <a href="/semantic-modelling/method">Ontology modelling</a>, and terminology has one home in the <a href="/glossary#ontology-modelling">canonical glossary</a>.</p>
  <p>Already reviewing material? <a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute">Review and contribute</a> turns these distinctions into definition, diagram, evidence, vocabulary and rule reviews. To see how the different views fit one agreement first, read <a href="/semantic-modelling/understand/what-we-are-building">What we are building</a>.</p>
</ModellingLayout>
